@page
@using OutPost2.Web.Pages
@model PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "What Outpost holds";
}

@*
    Written as a factual account of what the code actually does, not as a legal policy.
    Every claim here is checkable against the source, and if the behaviour changes this
    page is wrong and has to change with it. That is the only kind of privacy page worth
    having on something that custodies other people's credentials.
*@

<div class="op-pagehead">
    <div>
        <h1 class="op-h1">What Outpost holds</h1>
        <p class="op-lede">
            A description of what this instance stores and where, accurate to the code
            running it. Not a legal policy.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="op-group" aria-labelledby="h-creds">
    <div class="op-group__head"><h2 class="op-h2" id="h-creds">Credentials</h2></div>
    <div class="op-panel">
        <p style="margin:0">
            Every credential is encrypted before it is written down, with a key Outpost
            never sees in full: a data key is fetched per credential from HashiCorp Vault's
            transit engine, used once, and wiped from memory. What lands in the database is
            the sealed blob and the wrapped key.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0">
            That means a copy of the database on its own is not enough to read your mail.
            It also means that if Vault is lost, so is every credential &mdash; by design.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="op-group" aria-labelledby="h-tokens">
    <div class="op-group__head"><h2 class="op-h2" id="h-tokens">Tokens issued to Claude</h2></div>
    <div class="op-panel">
        <p style="margin:0">
            Access and refresh tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes, never in the clear.
            Outpost can check a token you present; it cannot produce one, and neither can
            anyone reading the database.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0">
            Revoking from <a asp-page="/ConnectedApps">Connected apps</a> takes effect on the
            very next request, and kills the refresh token with it.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="op-group" aria-labelledby="h-content">
    <div class="op-group__head"><h2 class="op-h2" id="h-content">Your mail, issues and code</h2></div>
    <div class="op-panel">
        <p style="margin:0">
            Not stored. Messages, issues and pull requests are fetched from the provider
            when asked for, passed to the conversation, and kept nowhere.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0">
            The exceptions are deliberate and visible: files you upload on the
            <a asp-page="/Files">Files</a> page, which expire on their own and can be deleted
            sooner, and the <a asp-page="/Jobs">Approvals</a> queue, which keeps a short
            record of what was sent so you can check afterwards.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="op-group" aria-labelledby="h-github">
    <div class="op-group__head"><h2 class="op-h2" id="h-github">Signing in</h2></div>
    <div class="op-panel">
        <p style="margin:0">
            GitHub sign-in asks for no scopes at all. It establishes who you are and nothing
            else: Outpost cannot read your repositories through it. What is kept is your
            GitHub user id, your login, and your avatar URL.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0">
            Access is assigned. An account that is not on the allowlist is refused before any
            record of it is created.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="op-group" aria-labelledby="h-preview">
    <div class="op-group__head"><h2 class="op-h2" id="h-preview">Preview links</h2></div>
    <div class="op-panel">
        <p style="margin:0">
            A <a asp-page="/PreviewLinks">preview link</a> acts as you, read-only, until it
            expires or you revoke it. The first address and browser that opens one is recorded
            and shown to you, so a link used from somewhere unexpected is visible afterwards.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
